Southern University at Shreveport Chancellor, Aubra J. Gantt joined fellow chancellors from across the Southern University System for the “One System, Many Campuses: Leadership, Vision, and the Future of Southern University System” panel during the 2026 SU Alumni Federation National Conference held in Dallas.
&nbsp;
Together, they highlighted a shared commitment to collaboration, innovation, and student success across all campuses.
&nbsp;
<div class="row">
<div class="col-md-6"><img src="/assets/susla/Newsroom/July-Sept2026/01.png" alt="Dr. Aubra J. Gantt holding mic, speaking at conference." width="100%" height="auto"></div>
<div class="col-md-6"><img src="/assets/susla/Newsroom/July-Sept2026/02.png" alt="Dr. Aubra J. Gantt holding mic, speaking at conference." width="100%" height="auto"></div>
</div>
&nbsp;
&nbsp;

Chef Gabriel Balderas partnered with the Louisiana Small Business Development Center (LSBDC) at Southern University at Shreveport to turn his vision for The Brine into a market-ready concept. Scheduled to open in late summer 2026 on the Louisiana Boardwalk in Bossier City, The Brine is a contemporary Southern restaurant and bar inside an indoor pickleball and health club, offering cocktails, smoothies, fresh crudo, oysters, prime meats, and panoramic Red River views.
&nbsp;
Although Balderas brought years of restaurant experience, launching a new brand required rigorous business planning and investor-ready materials. The LSBDC, led by Director Amanda Flynn, worked directly with Balderas to refine the business proposal, produce detailed financial projections, conduct industry research, and assemble a professional investor package. That guidance validated the concept, strengthened financial assumptions, and bolstered investor confidence.
&nbsp;
The Brine represents a 1 million dollar investment in the local economy and is expected to create 21 jobs, underscoring the LSBDC’s role in supporting small business growth and regional economic development. By providing practical research, financing strategies, and planning support, the LSBDC at SUSLA helps entrepreneurs move from concept to reality and strengthens the broader Shreveport-Bossier business ecosystem.

Congratulations to Kylie Mitchell, the 2026 recipient of the $1,000 Beverly Wallace Scholarship!
&nbsp;
Southern University at Shreveport’s Health Information Technology (H.I.T.) program honored her at its annual alumni reception, where the Beverly Wallace Scholarship is presented. This year’s award was presented by Lakendria Combs, the 2024 recipient, to Kylie Mitchell, who received a $1,000 scholarship. Mitchell reflected on her journey: “I’m honored to receive this scholarship. If you told me in 2014, as a 20‑year‑old, that I would be receiving it, I wouldn’t have believed it. I also wouldn’t have believed I’d be this close to obtaining my degree, which motivated me to return to school. Up until February of this year, I’ve been able to go to school, work a 9‑to‑5, pursue my hobbies, and still be a great mom.”
&nbsp;
After graduating from SUSLA, Mitchell plans to pursue a bachelor’s degree in Health Information Management Systems at Southern University at New Orleans (SUNO).
The Beverly Wallace Scholarship honors the legacy of Ms. Beverly Wallace, a beloved H.I.T. instructor whose dedication and leadership left a lasting impact on students and colleagues. Faculty, staff, students, HIT alumni, members of Wallace’s family, and representatives from New Hope Missionary Baptist Church gathered to celebrate her contributions.
&nbsp;
The reception, hosted by LaChandra Wells (H.I.T. Program/Medical Coding Specialist) and Venus Ammons (Professional Practice Experience Coordinator/Instructor), highlighted SUSLA’s commitment to honoring its pioneers and supporting the next generation of healthcare professionals. Events like this exemplify SUSLA’s mission to empower students to achieve their educational and career goals.
